    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ac3fb116_53a9162d",
        "filename": "board/hyperdebug/gpio.c",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 456,
      "author": {
        "id": 1341996
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-02-02T00:55:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "True, the current code runs the risk that an event with a timestamp later than our \"now\" will be added before we retrieve \"head\", and be reported on the list.\n\nIf we switch around the two lines, as I try to explain in the comment, we instead run the risk that an event added after head is retrieved but with a timestamp BEFORE our \"now\" will sit in the queue for next time.  I think this latter scenario is quite a bit worse, because it contradicts the assumption of the caller that the levels of each of the signals remain \"constant\" from the time of the last edge reported until the timestamp of the reading (our \"now\").\n\nThinking about it, it should be possible to prevent both issues, if we continue retrieving now before head, as is, but during the iteration through the buffer, stop either when we reach head, or when we reach an event with a timestamp later than now (whichever comes first.)",
      "parentUuid": "6d84ae51_d3a5ca3a",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 454,
        "startChar": 55,
        "endLine": 456,
        "endChar": 31
      },
      "revId": "05ede497d67a8f3949b03822cb6f1b229cc82954",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "491b9abb_ffffa98d",
        "filename": "board/hyperdebug/gpio.c",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 465,
      "author": {
        "id": 1259838
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-02-01T22:10:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "this condition will be false for a overrun buffer as well. Should we still print out everything it had?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 465,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 465,
        "endChar": 23
      },
      "revId": "05ede497d67a8f3949b03822cb6f1b229cc82954",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0f5c471a_6dfa6cbd",
        "filename": "board/hyperdebug/gpio.c",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 465,
      "author": {
        "id": 1341996
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-02-02T00:55:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Actually, the interrupt handler takes care to never update head so that it becomes equal to tail.  If that would have happened, it discards the record it wanted to insert, and instead sets the overflow flag.  So the condition here will be true for a buffer that has experienced overflow, causing its contents (the oldest events in the window) to be dumped, with an \"Error: Overflow\" message in the end.\n\nThinking about it.  Once an overflow has happened, and the data has a gap, we should stop adding any more events to the buffer, even if enough space is made later.  The current code disables further interrupt handling only on the one signal that contributed the last straw, but allows other pins to continue adding events.\n\nI suppose I could make the overflow() function iterate over all the slots, and disable every one that points to the buffer in question.",
      "parentUuid": "491b9abb_ffffa98d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 465,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 465,
        "endChar": 23
      },
      "revId": "05ede497d67a8f3949b03822cb6f1b229cc82954",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
